# Writer-starvation soak evidence (2026-09-28, shared local rig)
#
#   scripts/soak_starvation.py --blocks 6 --seconds 90 \
#       --readers 12 --writers 4 --scan-rows 12000
#
# Question (scale proposal Phase 2): does a continuous read flood on ONE
# database delay a writer enough to justify a writer-preferring bound in
# Store::acquire_queue?  Answer: YES, and the bound is built and measured.
#
#   verdict: STARVED before, NO_STARVATION after. Headline A/B below.
#
# Why docs/soak-2h-evidence.log cannot answer this: that run round-robins
# across four databases while acquire_queue takes a PER-DATABASE guard, so
# the contended lock is never actually contended. 1.28M requests and 0
# errors proves nothing about starvation. This harness inverts it: every
# reader and writer hammers ONE hot database, and an idle `cold` database
# is reported beside it so the per-database counters are visibly
# per-database rather than global (it reads all zeros in every run).
#
# The arms are DIFFERENTIAL and INTERLEAVED. This rig is shared and its load
# moved between 14 and 127 over the day, so an absolute latency threshold
# would measure the neighbours. Blocks alternate control/flood/control/
# flood/... at equal duration, so drift lands in both arms and only the read
# flood separates them; every block records the load it ran at. The verdict
# is the writer REJECTION RATE, not p99: at high load both arms sit on the
# 500ms ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT ceiling where p99 saturates and stops discriminating.
#
# Attribution is checkable, not assumed. A queue timeout also lands in the
# global requests.capacity_exceeded, so the summary reports the lane-shed
# remainder; it is 0 in every run here, which proves the rejections measured
# are per-database queue timeouts and not global-lane shedding. --writers
# stays at 4, inside WRITE_PERMITS=4, so no writer is ever lane-shed.
#
# --scan-rows is the knob that makes this measurement honest. The range-scan
# width sets how long a reader holds the shared guard (~135ms at 12,000
# rows), and therefore whether the flood is a real flood. A client that
# leaves a gap between requests lets a writer in through the gap regardless
# of what the queue does, which is how the first attempt at this evidence
# produced a false NO_STARVATION -- see "the measurement that was wrong".

## headline A/B: the same flood, before and after the bound

Identical harness, identical parameters (12 readers, 4 writers, 12000-row
scans), same machine, one session:

| | pre-fix polled RwLock | post-fix ticket lock |
|---|---|---|
| verdict | STARVED | NO_STARVATION |
| writer rejection, control | 0.143% | 0.086% |
| writer rejection, FLOOD | 35.298% | 0.014% |
| flood / control | x247.6 | x0.2 |
| write throughput, control | 60.8/s | 61.0/s |
| write throughput, FLOOD | 12.1/s | 54.7/s |
| per-db queue timeouts, FLOOD | 1148 | 3 |
| reader rejections, FLOOD | 16 | 1 |
| writer p50, control / flood | 46.75 / 373.65ms | 48.1 / 56.65ms |
| mean machine load, ctl/fld | 28.8 / 33.0 | 22.9 / 23.6 |
| matched pairs separating | 3/3 | 1/3 |

The read flood raised the writer rejection rate by **247x** on the old queue
(0.143%% -> 35.298%%), cost 80%% of write throughput (60.8/s -> 12.1/s) and
produced 1,148 queue timeouts. On the shipped ticket lock the same flood
moved it 0.086%% -> 0.014%%, throughput fell 10%%, and the flood produced 3
queue timeouts and 1 reader rejection. All 3 matched block pairs separate in
the pre-fix flood's disfavour and none do after, at block loads that agree
closely (pre-fix 22/34, 30/27, 34/38; post-fix 22/27, 23/29, 24/16).

## the measurement that was wrong

An earlier version of this log reported NO_STARVATION for a design that was
wrong, and the reason is worth keeping. That design closed the read phase
for a queued writer and then decided who owned the queue with:

    self.writer_phase = self.waiting_readers == 0 && self.writer_serving < self.writer_next;

Under a genuine read flood `waiting_readers` is essentially never zero, so
the read phase was re-opened for the readers at every batch boundary and a
queued writer was never preferred. It was a mutex race, not a bound, and its
safety depended on the CLIENT's request pacing: the Python harness leaves a
gap between requests, in which `waiting_readers` hits zero and a writer slips
in. The verdict was measuring the client, not the queue.

Caught in-repo, not taken on report: with 8 readers (the server's own
READ_PERMITS) re-acquiring with no gap and 2 writers, that queue admitted
**0 of 6 writers, all 6 timing out at ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT**. The soak could not
see it because of the client gap.

The shipped design is a ticket lock with no phases to race. `serving` names
the only ticket allowed to proceed, and a reader may enter only while no
writer holds a smaller ticket. Because tickets are handed out on arrival,
the readers already inside plus those arriving before the writer form one
finite batch, and no reader arriving after the writer can get in ahead of
it. The bound is a property of the queue, not of the client.

## the other rejected design, kept on the record

A writer-preferring flag over the original RwLock was implemented first and
measured (MARGINAL, commit 223e7c7, 2 writers):

| | control | flood |
|---|---|---|
| writer rejection | 0.061% | 0.075% |
| write throughput | 37.2/s | 35.0/s |
| per-db queue timeouts | 6 | 4267 |
| reader rejections | 0 | 4260 |

It fixed the writers by 9.3x and starved the readers: 4,260 reader
rejections against 7 writer rejections, 47x more total timeouts. A bound that
moves the queue sideways is not a bound, which is what led to the ticket
lock. Recorded because it is the tempting wrong answer.

