## isolated stress: the bound, with no client in the way

The soak drives the real server over HTTP, which leaves a gap between
requests, and that gap is exactly what let the phase design look healthy. So
the queue was also stressed directly, with the admission code copied verbatim
and no network at all: 8 reader threads (the server's READ_PERMITS) that
re-acquire immediately with NO client-side gap, 4 writer threads, 6 seconds
per case. The only variable is the reader's hold time.

| reader hold | phase design: writers admitted / rejected | ticket lock: writers admitted / rejected |
|---|---|---|
| 2ms  | 0 / 48 (**100% rejected**) | 4,380 / 0 (0%) |
| 20ms | 0 / 48 (**100% rejected**) | 896 / 0 (0%) |
| 45ms | 0 / 48 (**100% rejected**) | 313 / 0 (0%) |

Readers were never rejected by either design (0 at every hold), which is the
whole point: the phase design was not losing throughput, it was refusing to
admit writers at all, and it did so while looking perfectly healthy to a
paced client. The ticket lock admits every writer at every hold.

`abandoned` drained to 0 in all six cases, so the give-up accounting does not
accumulate and the queue does not wedge behind a shed ticket.

This also answers the sharpest open question about the bound: a writer's wait
is bounded by the readers already inside it, so the long-hold case is the
adversarial one. Eight readers at 45ms is 360ms of worst-case drain, still
inside ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT=500ms, and the measured result is zero timeouts.

